  "mother": {
    "preparation": {
      "model_calls": 1,
      "phase": "preparation",
      "plan": {
        "A": "Investigate source-level parallelism across independent target bodies in `body_force`, plus the independent position-update loop, using at most the target's 4 CPU cores. Preserve each body's serial `j` accumulation order and ensure all force updates finish before positions change. Keep the existing compiler/toolchain unchanged; if the available build does not support the proposed threading, report that rather than changing it. Measure end-to-end time and correctness.",
        "B": "Investigate per-interaction arithmetic improvements, starting with whether a single-precision square-root/reciprocal path is faster than the current `sqrt` double-precision path on ARM64. Check every candidate against the correctness oracle because changed rounding can affect results; reject any that fail. Avoid approximate math or altered summation order unless correctness is demonstrated, and do not change compiler settings.",
        "C": "Explore a data-layout and memory-access candidate: stage coordinates (and, if useful, velocities) in structure-of-arrays form to make the O(n²) force loop more contiguous and amenable to existing compiler vectorization, then restore the required `Body` binary output layout. Measure conversion overhead as part of the full run and verify correctness, especially if vectorization changes reduction order. Do not use pairwise-symmetry updates that introduce races or different accumulation semantics.",
        "G": "Integrate only candidates produced by A, B, and C. Use the baseline as a fallback and measure each candidate and promising combination on the unchanged harness: combinations may be slower than their parts. Require correctness across the full supplied case set before selecting an integration; preserve binary I/O and the simulation's iteration count and semantics. Compare against the measured baseline mean of 0.662252 seconds on the 4-core ARM64 target, without specializing to test inputs.",
        "analysis": "The measured baseline is valid on all 4 cases, with mean time 0.662252 seconds and score 0.5. The simulation performs ten O(n²) all-pairs force passes, making the force kernel the main optimization opportunity. The three research objectives separate low-risk parallelism, arithmetic, and data layout; each requires end-to-end measurement and correctness checks because numerical changes and combined gains are not guaranteed."
      },
